WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
We've all heard it, and most of us have done it.

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:12.100
And yet for seventy years, scientists couldn't say for certain what exactly bursts inside the joint to make that little "crack," or whether anything bursts at all.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:20.230
The answer was found by slowly pulling a finger inside an MRI scanner, and it overturned the explanation everyone thought they already knew.

4
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.880
Interlace your fingers, turn your palms outward, and push.

5
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.930
In the quiet of the room comes that familiar sound, dry and sharp, like a small twig snapping.

6
00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.580
Perhaps you've already done it today without noticing.

7
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:49.270
Before a meeting began, before an exam paper was set in front of you, at the end of a long hour at the computer.

8
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.560
A considerable share of the world's population has this habit.

9
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:59.450
Some do it to relax, some when they're tense, and some simply without realizing they're doing it.

10
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:04.860
But there's a question worth stopping to ask: where exactly does that sound come from?

11
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.190
No bone is breaking, we know that much.

12
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.560
No muscle is tearing.

13
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.170
The finger is the same one second later as it was one second before.

14
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:21.700
And yet something happens so fast and so forcefully that the person next to you might turn their head to look.

15
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:28.710
The strange part is that the source of such an ordinary, everyday sound divided scientists for nearly seventy years.

16
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.880
First, let's set the scene.

17
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:38.890
Take one of the joints in your finger, say the one that sits like a knob on the back of your hand and connects the finger to the palm.

18
00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.180
It is really a meeting point where the ends of two bones face each other.

19
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:48.750
These bone ends are covered in cartilage, so they never grind directly against one another.

20
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.240
Around them is a sealed pouch called the joint capsule.

21
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.810
Inside, the capsule is filled with a slippery fluid much like egg white.

22
00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:02.740
This is called joint fluid, or in medical terms, synovial fluid.

23
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.470
Its job is to lubricate the joint, nourish the cartilage, and keep movement smooth.

24
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:14.360
One property of this fluid shapes the entire story: it carries dissolved gases.

25
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.770
Think of a sealed bottle of soda, where carbon dioxide sits mixed into the liquid.

26
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:24.860
You don't see bubbles in the bottle, because pressure holds the gas within the liquid.

27
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:30.430
The moment you open the cap, the pressure drops, and the gas reveals itself as bubbles.

28
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.640
Now picture the joint.

29
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.090
When you pull or bend your finger, the two bone ends are forced apart.

30
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.700
But because the capsule is sealed, no fluid can flow in from outside.

31
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.310
The volume increases while the amount of fluid inside stays the same.

32
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:50.920
The result is that the pressure inside the capsule drops rapidly.

33
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:58.250
At a certain threshold, the pressure falls so low that the fluid can no longer hold together, and a cavity opens up within it.

34
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.420
Physicists call this cavitation.

35
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:08.750
The same phenomenon appears behind ship propellers, which push water so quickly that vapor-filled cavities form in their wake.

36
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:17.040
The first to show that this cavity really forms inside the joint were two researchers working in Manchester, England, in nineteen forty-seven: J.

37
00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.450
B.

38
00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.660
Roston and R.

39
00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.190
Wheeler Haines.

40
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:29.560
They built an apparatus that gradually pulled on volunteers' fingers, taking X-rays at every stage.

41
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.490
As the pulling force increased, the joint resisted for a while.

42
00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:39.580
Then came the sound, and at the same instant the two bone ends suddenly sprang apart.

43
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:45.790
In the X-ray taken after the sound, a dark area appeared inside the joint where none had been before.

44
00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.280
It was a gas-filled cavity.

45
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:55.810
X-rays pass with difficulty through dense tissue such as bone, which is why bones show up light on the film.

46
00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.900
Gas, on the other hand, barely stops the rays at all and leaves a dark patch.

47
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.630
The patch Roston and Haines saw was the shadow of a bubble born in an instant inside the joint.

48
00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:17.000
To them, the cause of the sound was clear: the sudden opening of the cavity, the rapid separation of the joint surfaces, and that separation setting the surrounding tissues vibrating.

49
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.650
In short, the sound was the moment of the bubble's birth.

50
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:23.500
This explanation seemed sensible.

51
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:30.670
But more than twenty years later, another team would look at the very same X-ray images and reach an entirely different conclusion.

52
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:38.320
And the dispute between these two views could only be settled by a technology that made it possible to watch the inside of the joint in real time.

53
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.690
In nineteen seventy-one, an engineering team at the University of Leeds in England took up the question again.

54
00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:52.180
Anthony Unsworth, Duncan Dowson and Verna Wright approached the joint not as a biology problem but as a mechanical system.

55
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:57.590
This time, they added sound recording and force measurement to their finger-pulling apparatus.

56
00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:03.640
They studied the waveform of the sound and estimated the composition of the gas that collected in the joint.

57
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:08.570
According to their findings, this gas was largely carbon dioxide.

58
00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:13.980
The Leeds team offered a different interpretation from their predecessors in Manchester.

59
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.070
In their view, the sound came not from the bubble's birth but from its collapse.

60
00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:25.920
Their scenario went like this: the joint is pulled, the pressure drops, and a bubble forms in the fluid.

61
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:33.250
Then, as the joint capsule stretches and flexes, the pressure begins to rebalance and the bubble collapses in on itself.

62
00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:38.500
This collapse creates a shock wave, and the crack we hear is actually that wave.

63
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.110
This idea rested on a well-known phenomenon in the physics of cavitation.

64
00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:51.560
The bubbles that form behind a ship's propeller produce shock waves so powerful when they collapse that, over time, they can wear pits into solid metal.

65
00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.010
The Leeds team believed something similar was happening inside the joint.

66
00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:01.900
Their interpretation proved so convincing that in the decades that followed it made its way into the textbooks.

67
00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:09.870
The notion that a knuckle crack was a bubble bursting came to be accepted almost without question, both in medical training and among the general public.

68
00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:14.160
Another of the Leeds team's observations still holds true today.

69
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.450
Once you have cracked a joint, you cannot crack it again right away.

70
00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:23.220
You have to wait a while, and the team measured that interval at roughly twenty minutes.

71
00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:31.190
You may have noticed this yourself: after cracking a finger once, no matter how hard you try, the second pop simply will not come.

72
00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:36.760
During this waiting period, it was thought, the gas had to dissolve back into the fluid.

73
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:42.970
In other words, the joint was waiting for the cap to be screwed back on the soda bottle and for the bottle to settle.

74
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.980
But one knot remained untied.

75
00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:55.710
If the sound was the collapse of a bubble, and the bubble had collapsed and vanished, then what was the gas-filled space that Roston and Haines had seen on their X-rays after the sound?

76
00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:02.080
According to the Leeds team, it might be smaller bubbles left behind once the large one had collapsed.

77
00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.650
Both explanations fit the data in part, and neither could definitively rule out the other.

78
00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:11.940
The problem was this: an X-ray gives you only snapshots.

79
00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:20.070
There was no way to see what was happening inside the joint at the exact split second of the sound, or precisely when the bubble appeared or disappeared.

80
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:24.200
Untying that knot would have to wait until twenty fifteen.

81
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:35.050
A team led by Greg Kawchuk, a physiotherapist and researcher at the University of Alberta in Canada, decided to film the inside of the joint at the very moment the sound was produced.

82
00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.820
The tool they used was magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI.

83
00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:47.470
But this was no ordinary MRI scan; they used a dynamic imaging method capable of capturing several frames per second.

84
00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:56.400
About three frames per second may seem painfully slow by today's video standards, but for watching the inside of a joint in real time, it was a first.

85
00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.690
The hardest part of the experiment was finding the right volunteer.

86
00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:04.340
Not everyone can crack their knuckles whenever they please.

87
00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:12.310
The team worked with someone who could crack his joints almost on demand: the chiropractor Jerome Fryer, one of the study's co-authors.

88
00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:17.240
Fryer's fingers were placed, one at a time, inside a flexible tube.

89
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:21.530
The tube was attached to a cable that ran out of the MRI machine.

90
00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:30.140
The researchers pulled the cable slowly, with steady force, and the finger was stretched inside the MRI, right before the camera's eye.

91
00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:34.430
The images reopened a debate that had lasted for decades.

92
00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:39.680
As the pulling force increased, the two ends of the joint stayed stuck together for a time.

93
00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.250
Then came the sound, and at that very instant a dark cavity appeared inside the joint.

94
00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.740
This cavity did not vanish after the sound.

95
00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.550
On the contrary, it persisted.

96
00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:58.760
In other words, the sound coincided not with the bubble's disappearance, but with its appearance.

97
00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:03.850
Kawchuk and his team explained this through a process called tribonucleation.

98
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:07.660
It may sound complicated, but the idea is simple.

99
00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:12.430
Imagine pressing two wet panes of glass together and then trying to pull them apart.

100
00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:17.200
The thin layer of liquid between them holds the panes together with surprising strength.

101
00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.610
They resist up to a certain force, and then, all at once, they separate.

102
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:27.860
At that moment of separation, a cavity rapidly opens in the liquid between them.

103
00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.870
Joint surfaces behave the same way.

104
00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:37.880
The adhesion created by the slippery fluid builds up tension as the finger is pulled, until it is suddenly released.

105
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:42.490
A cavity is born, and the sound is born along with it.

106
00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:49.500
This result seemed to support the interpretation put forward by the first team, in Manchester, in nineteen forty-seven.

107
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.350
But the story did not end there either.

108
00:09:52.350 --> 00:10:03.040
In twenty eighteen, Vineeth Chandran Suja of Stanford University and Abdul Barakat of the École Polytechnique in France approached the problem with pen and paper, that is, with mathematics.

109
00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:10.210
They built equations that took into account the geometry of the joint, the properties of the fluid, and the changes in pressure.

110
00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:15.940
Then they checked whether the sound these equations predicted matched real recordings of knuckle cracking.

111
00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.430
Their conclusion surprised both camps.

112
00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:24.040
According to their model, the sound might come from the partial collapse of the bubble.

113
00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:32.810
That is, the bubble forms, part of it collapses immediately afterward, and that collapse produces the sound, while the rest of the bubble remains in the joint.

114
00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:42.700
This was a proposed compromise, one that could explain within a single framework both why the cavity remained visible on the MRI after the sound and the Leeds team's idea of collapse.

115
00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:49.870
Here is where things stand today: scientists agree that the sound is linked to the sudden formation of a cavity in the joint fluid.

116
00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:58.800
But whether the moment that produces the sound is the opening of that cavity itself, or the collapse of part of it immediately afterward, is still debated.

117
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:07.730
Settling the question for certain will require imaging far faster than anything MRI can capture, because the entire event unfolds within a few milliseconds.

118
00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:13.140
In other words, the final word on this most ordinary of sounds has yet to be spoken.

119
00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:18.710
So let's set aside the source of the sound and turn to the question on everyone's mind.

120
00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:28.600
Perhaps, as a child, an older relative told you something like this: Stop cracking your knuckles, or your joints will wear out when you grow up and you'll end up with arthritis.

121
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:34.650
That warning has been handed down from generation to generation, and many people still believe it.

122
00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:37.660
So what does science have to say?

123
00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:43.870
One of the most unusual answers to this question came from a California physician named Donald Unger.

124
00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:50.240
Unger recalled that as a child, his mother, his aunts and other elders gave him exactly this warning.

125
00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:55.490
But he wondered whether they were right, and he turned his own body into an experiment.

126
00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:01.860
For more than sixty years, at least twice a day, he cracked the knuckles of his left hand only.

127
00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:05.030
His right hand he almost never cracked.

128
00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.360
The right hand became the experiment's control group.

129
00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:15.370
By Unger's own count, the knuckles of his left hand had been cracked at least thirty-six thousand times over that period.

130
00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:23.820
When he compared his two hands years later, he found no sign of arthritis in either one, that is, no joint inflammation and no joint degeneration.

131
00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.830
There was no noticeable difference between them.

132
00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:32.400
He shared this result in nineteen ninety-eight, in a short letter to a rheumatology journal.

133
00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:38.930
In it, he noted that this one-person experiment, at least in his own case, did not support his elders' warning.

134
00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:49.940
Then, in two thousand nine, this stubborn and witty piece of work earned him the Ig Nobel Prize in Medicine, an award that honors research that first makes people laugh, and then makes them think.

135
00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:55.670
Unger's experiment is a charming story, but its scientific limitations are obvious.

136
00:12:55.670 --> 00:13:00.440
A single person, a single hand, a single observer.

137
00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:06.810
Perhaps Unger was genetically resistant to arthritis; perhaps in someone else the result would have been different.

138
00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:11.100
That is why the real weight of evidence lies in studies of larger groups.

139
00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:17.630
In a study published in nineteen ninety, Jorge Castellanos and David Axelrod examined three hundred people.

140
00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:23.040
They found no difference in the rate of arthritis between those who cracked their knuckles and those who didn't.

141
00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:29.570
They did, however, report that habitual knuckle crackers had somewhat more hand swelling and reduced grip strength.

142
00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:38.660
Interesting as this finding was, it was not consistently replicated in later studies, and it remained unclear whether the relationship was one of cause and effect.

143
00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:45.990
People whose hands are already tense or tired, for example, might simply crack their knuckles more often to find relief.

144
00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:53.800
Then, in twenty eleven, Kevin deWeber and his colleagues studied two hundred fifteen people who had had hand X-rays taken.

145
00:13:53.800 --> 00:14:02.730
They asked the participants how often, and for how many years, they had cracked their knuckles, and then looked at the X-rays for signs of degeneration in the hand joints.

146
00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:10.060
Once again, the result was the same: no meaningful link was found between knuckle cracking and degeneration of the hand joints.

147
00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:16.430
Even among people who had cracked their knuckles a great many times over the years, there was no increase in risk.

148
00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:21.200
There is also a sensible explanation behind this consistent picture.

149
00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:31.410
Joint degeneration, known in medical terms as osteoarthritis, is a disease that develops as the cartilage in a joint wears down over the years, and as the joint responds to that wear.

150
00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:40.020
It is associated with factors such as age, genetic predisposition, excess weight, old injuries and prolonged heavy loading of the joint.

151
00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:45.590
When you crack your knuckles, though, the bones don't strike each other and the cartilage isn't crushed.

152
00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:51.320
What happens is a change in pressure within the joint fluid, followed by the formation of a cavity.

153
00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:55.610
That is not the kind of mechanical stress that wears away cartilage.

154
00:14:55.610 --> 00:15:01.180
But this doesn't mean that knuckle cracking is completely insignificant under all circumstances.

155
00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:11.710
If you feel pain when cracking a joint, or if the joint swells or its movement becomes restricted, the issue may not be the sound itself, but some other problem that the sound accompanies,

156
00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:15.360
such as a ligament injury or damage inside the joint.

157
00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:21.090
In that case, what deserves attention is not the habit itself, but the cause of the pain.

158
00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:28.740
Painless cracking sounds, whether they happen on their own or on purpose, are considered harmless according to the current evidence.

159
00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:32.710
Then there is the question of why cracking feels so relieving.

160
00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:38.760
Many people say that after cracking, they feel a loosening, a lightness in the joint.

161
00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:42.730
The exact mechanism behind this is not fully understood.

162
00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:50.700
One possibility is that when the joint capsule is stretched, nearby nerve endings are stimulated, creating a brief sense of relaxation.

163
00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:55.950
Another is that the joint's range of motion increases slightly for a while after cracking.

164
00:15:55.950 --> 00:16:02.960
The waiting period of roughly twenty minutes measured by the Leeds team suggests that this loosening may also last for some time.

165
00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:09.650
These interpretations, however, are not yet settled, and researchers present them only as possibilities.

166
00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:17.620
Perhaps the most valuable thing the story of knuckle cracking leaves us with is not so much the source of the sound as a lesson in how science works.

167
00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:24.630
An explanation that one generation wrote into textbooks as established fact can be questioned anew once a better tool comes along.

168
00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:37.240
From the X-ray films in Manchester to the sound recordings in Leeds, from the MRI images in Alberta to the equations at Stanford, each step sharpened the question left behind by the one before, but never fully closed it.

169
00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:45.370
Likewise, a warning handed down from generation to generation revealed its true worth only when someone took it seriously and put it to the test.

170
00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:56.060
Donald Unger's quiet comparison between his left hand and his right, and the studies involving hundreds of people that followed, remind us that our intuitions are not always reliable guides.

171
00:16:56.060 --> 00:17:04.670
A sound that seems harmful can turn out to be harmless, while a phenomenon that sounds simple can grow into a scientific debate lasting more than half a century.

172
00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:11.200
And now, the next time you interlace your fingers and push, you'll know what lies behind that brief sound.

173
00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:15.970
The slippery fluid inside the joint gives up its resistance for just an instant.

174
00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:24.900
The pressure drops, a cavity is born, and the echo of that birth, and perhaps of a partial collapse that follows right after, reaches your ear.

175
00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:35.270
Lasting mere milliseconds, this event is a tiny physics experiment that, even today, has not been fully solved, and it repeats itself every day in millions of hands.

176
00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:38.920
We've reached the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

177
00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:42.090
Until we meet again in a new story.
